      "text": "We need to find the smallest possible value of \\( n \\) such that \\( n \\equiv 3 \\pmod{4} \\) and \\( n \\equiv 2 \\pmod{5} \\), with \\( n > 10 \\).\n\nFirst, let's write the congruences:\n\\[ n \\equiv 3 \\pmod{4} \\]\n\\[ n \\equiv 2 \\pmod{5} \\]\n\nFrom the first congruence, we can express \\( n \\) in the form:\n\\[ n = 4k + 3 \\]\nfor some integer \\( k \\).\n\nSubstituting this expression into the second congruence, we get:\n\\[ 4k + 3 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{5} \\]\n\\[ 4k + 3 - 2 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{5} \\]\n\\[ 4k + 1 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{5} \\]\n\\[ 4k \\equiv -1 \\pmod{5} \\]\n\\[ 4k \\equiv 4 \\pmod{5} \\]\nSince 4 and 5 are coprime, we can divide both sides by 4:\n\\[ k \\equiv 1 \\pmod{5} \\]\n\\[ k = 5m + 1 \\]\nfor some integer \\( m \\).\n\nSubstituting \\( k = 5m + 1 \\) back into the expression for \\( n \\), we get:\n\\[ n = 4(5m + 1) + 3 \\]\n\\[ n = 20m + 4 + 3 \\]\n\\[ n = 20m + 7 \\]\n\nSo, \\( n \\) can be written as:\n\\[ n = 20m + 7 \\]\n\nWe need the smallest value of \\( n \\) that is greater than 10. Let's find the smallest \\( m \\) such that \\( n > 10 \\):\n\\[ 20m + 7 > 10 \\]\n\\[ 20m > 3 \\]\n\\[ m > \\frac{3}{20} \\]\nSince \\( m \\) is an integer, the smallest possible value for \\( m \\) is 1.\n\nSubstituting \\( m = 1 \\) into the expression for \\( n \\), we get:\n\\[ n = 20(1) + 7 = 27 \\]\n\nTherefore, the smallest possible value of \\( n \\) that is greater than 10 is:\n\\[ \\boxed{27} \\]",
