      "text": "To solve the problem, we need to determine the smallest possible value of \\( n \\) that satisfies the given conditions when Ray climbs the stairs 4 steps at a time and 5 steps at a time. Let's break down the problem step by step.\n\n1. **Condition for climbing 4 steps at a time:**\n   When Ray climbs 4 steps at a time, there are 3 steps left at the top. This can be expressed as:\n   \\[\n   n \\equiv 3 \\pmod{4}\n   \\]\n   This means \\( n = 4k + 3 \\) for some integer \\( k \\).\n\n2. **Condition for climbing 5 steps at a time:**\n   When Ray climbs 5 steps at a time, there are 2 steps left at the top. This can be expressed as:\n   \\[\n   n \\equiv 2 \\pmod{5}\n   \\]\n   This means \\( n = 5m + 2 \\) for some integer \\( m \\).\n\nWe need to find the smallest \\( n > 10 \\) that satisfies both congruences. We will substitute the first expression into the second and find the appropriate \\( k \\) and \\( m \\).\n\nFirst, substitute \\( n = 4k + 3 \\) into the second congruence:\n\\[\n4k + 3 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{5}\n\\]\nSimplify the congruence:\n\\[\n4k + 3 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{5} \\implies 4k \\equiv -1 \\pmod{5} \\implies 4k \\equiv 4 \\pmod{5}\n\\]\nSince 4 and 5 are coprime, we can divide both sides by 4:\n\\[\nk \\equiv 1 \\pmod{5}\n\\]\nThis means \\( k = 5j + 1 \\) for some integer \\( j \\). Substitute \\( k = 5j + 1 \\) back into the expression for \\( n \\):\n\\[\nn = 4k + 3 = 4(5j + 1) + 3 = 20j + 4 + 3 = 20j + 7\n\\]\nSo, \\( n \\) can be written as:\n\\[\nn = 20j + 7\n\\]\nWe need the smallest \\( n > 10 \\):\n\\[\n20j + 7 > 10 \\implies 20j > 3 \\implies j > \\frac{3}{20}\n\\]\nThe smallest integer \\( j \\) that satisfies this inequality is \\( j = 1 \\). Substituting \\( j = 1 \\) into the expression for \\( n \\):\n\\[\nn = 20 \\cdot 1 + 7 = 27\n\\]\nTherefore, the smallest possible value of \\( n \\) that is greater than 10 is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{27}\n\\]",
