      "text": "Given the problem, we need to find the smallest integer \\( n > 10 \\) such that when Ray climbs the stairs in multiples of 4, there are 3 steps left at the top, and when he climbs the stairs in multiples of 5, there are 2 steps left at the top. This can be expressed mathematically as:\n\n\\[\nn \\equiv 3 \\pmod{4}\n\\]\n\\[\nn \\equiv 2 \\pmod{5}\n\\]\n\nWe start by expressing \\( n \\) in terms of the first congruence:\n\\[\nn = 4k + 3 \\quad \\text{for some integer } k\n\\]\n\nNext, we substitute this expression into the second congruence:\n\\[\n4k + 3 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{5}\n\\]\nSimplifying this, we get:\n\\[\n4k + 3 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{5}\n\\]\n\\[\n4k \\equiv -1 \\pmod{5}\n\\]\nSince \\(-1 \\equiv 4 \\pmod{5}\\), we have:\n\\[\n4k \\equiv 4 \\pmod{5}\n\\]\nDividing both sides by 4 (which is valid since 4 and 5 are coprime), we get:\n\\[\nk \\equiv 1 \\pmod{5}\n\\]\nThus, we can write \\( k \\) as:\n\\[\nk = 5m + 1 \\quad \\text{for some integer } m\n\\]\nSubstituting \\( k \\) back into the expression for \\( n \\):\n\\[\nn = 4(5m + 1) + 3\n\\]\n\\[\nn = 20m + 4 + 3\n\\]\n\\[\nn = 20m + 7\n\\]\n\nWe need the smallest \\( n > 10 \\):\n\\[\n20m + 7 > 10\n\\]\n\\[\n20m > 3\n\\]\n\\[\nm > \\frac{3}{20}\n\\]\nSince \\( m \\) is an integer, the smallest value for \\( m \\) is 1. Substituting \\( m = 1 \\):\n\\[\nn = 20 \\cdot 1 + 7 = 27\n\\]\n\nThus, the smallest possible value of \\( n \\) that is greater than 10 is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{27}\n\\]",
